00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.920
Now, here's something that most retail investors get wrong

00:07:55.520 --> 00:07:57.780
and it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:07:58.860 --> 00:08:02.200
Imagine you bought a Berkshire Hathaway position last quarter

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:04.540
after reading about its insurance operations.

00:08:05.380 --> 00:08:08.260
Your shares were acquired at the prevailing market price

00:08:08.260 --> 00:08:10.980
yet the real engine behind the long-term return

00:08:10.980 --> 00:08:13.060
wasn't the equity portfolio alone.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:16.940
It was the float generated by premiums collected upfront

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.680
and invested for years before claims were paid.

00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:24.220
In 2025, the float exceeded $170 billion,

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:28.060
effectively providing Berkshire with interest-free leverage

00:08:28.060 --> 00:08:31.300
that amplifies every percentage point of investment return.

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:34.180
Most retail investors overlook this

00:08:34.180 --> 00:08:36.540
because they focus only on the stock holdings

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.900
rather than the liability side of the balance sheet.

00:08:39.660 --> 00:08:41.680
The pro tip is to scan the footnotes

00:08:41.680 --> 00:08:45.280
of any insurance heavy company's 10K for the float line item

00:08:45.840 --> 00:08:48.220
and compare its growth rate to the investment yield.

00:08:49.060 --> 00:08:51.120
When float expands faster than claims,

00:08:51.500 --> 00:08:53.280
the compounding advantage widens.

00:08:53.820 --> 00:08:57.160
The biggest misconception is treating float as free money

00:08:57.160 --> 00:08:58.600
without duration risk.

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.100
Instead, always check the duration

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:03.640
of the insurance liabilities before assuming

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.940
the structure will work at smaller scales.

